<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Central Africa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> is the region of the African continent centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-basin">Congo basin</a>, including present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and neighbouring countries. It was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo Empire</a> near the Atlantic coast and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire</a> in the interior. Both were drawn into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> and later colonised by European powers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/central-africa.jpg" alt="Central Africa" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sannita · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UN_Macroregion_Central_Africa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Congo Free State was King Leopold II’s private colony in <b>Central Africa</b>, where forced collection of wild rubber caused mass death and suffering; the international campaign against it became a landmark of human rights activism, and its borders and methods shaped the Belgian Congo and the modern…</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom's rise was facilitated by fertile agricultural land, access to riverine trade routes and its position between the Atlantic coast and the interior of <b>Central Africa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kongo-empire">Kongo Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Luba Empire stands as one of <b>Central Africa</b>'s most influential states, whose model of sacred kingship, held in check by the Bambudye society, was adopted by neighbours such as the Lunda and outlived the civil war and colonial conquest that ended its independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/luba-empire">Luba Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lunda Empire exerted significant influence over <b>Central Africa</b>, both culturally and economically.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese and later Brazilian merchants became its principal organisers, drawing captives above all from West <b>Central Africa</b> through Luanda and other ports; Portuguese and Brazilian vessels are estimated to have carried some 5.8 million enslaved Africans across the Atlantic between the sixteenth and mid-nineteenth centuries, the majority to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Kerma stood between Egypt and the African interior and played an important role in passing luxury goods from <b>Central Africa</b> to Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kerma-culture">Kerma culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
